> the caveman is really just a Barbarian with crappier starting 
> inventory and a crappier first gift.

And a better Quest artifact, some actual spellcasting ability, a
reasonably different set of Quest levels...

The Valk is the one who's sort of just sitting there ATM.

> As for the knight, I've always felt that the starting horse and saddle
> and the code of conduct actually goes quite a surprisingly long way to
> successfully differentiating the knight.

...Now that you can actually ride your horse on turn 1, at least, and
the code of conduct matters more than "not at all".

>> While startscumming isn't productive in and of itself (if you can't
>> ascend without startscumming, you likely can't ascend _with_
>> startscumming), I still don't want to encourage it.
>
> through (like fountain quaffing). That being said, I do really like a
> certain amount of randomness in the starting inventory. There's
> something exciting about rolling an Archaeologist and turning up that
> marker.

That's exactly the problem.  A marker is _too good_ to be handed out at
random in starting inventory; that simply encourages startscumming,
which while generally unproductive still indicates that something is
wrong.

For reference, a quick browse of the "top wishes" on sporkhack.com shows:

#1 -- 231 magic markers
#2 -- 98 gray dragon scale mails

I don't mind there being a certain amount of randomness, but at the same
time a character's starting inventory should be more "just enough to
survive" as opposed to "stuff you'll use for the rest of the game."

>> No role in Spork will ever gain intrinsic magic resistance; in fact, I'd
>> like to see it become less common on the Quest Artifacts themselves, and
>> possibly more common in some other overloaded slots.  But the intrinsic
>> leads down a path I will not travel.
> Fair enough. I presume you did notice the caveat that these intrinsics
> are only available when wearing two or fewer pieces of armour, yes? 

I did notice, yes, and I still will not add MR as an intrinsic.  It's
not about the level, either; obtaining MR is one of the challenges.

>> ...which means that people will simply put on the ascension kit and have
>> done with it, because AC -16 isn't enough in Spork.  Weight isn't an issue
>> past the midgame.
> Well, the example left out divine protection. With 10 points of
> protection, you'd have -26 AC which is passable (or at least was the
> last time I ascended in Spork). 

Now we're down the road of _requiring_ divine protection though, and
that's fishy in a different sense.  (-26AC is possible, yes, but you'd
better have some HP backing it up.)

>> I like the idea of the Barbarian getting bonuses for not wearing armor,
>> but there is a major problem with that: so many magical armors grant
>> something useful that unless the Barbarian's bonuses make him
>> significantly _better_ than a standard Ascension Kit, people simply
>> aren't going to bother and are going to stick with the routine.
>
> Well, as I said above, I feel like there SHOULD be a suite of non-
> armour bonuses which would result in a barb so outfitted being only
> SLIGHTLY weaker than if he were in a full ascension kit. 

...meaning players will just use the ascension kit.

To properly confuse players and force them into a decision, the
non-armour bonuses should be slightly _better_ than the ascension kit,
thus leaving players in the "familiar AK, or throw out all my status
effects and take a totally different approach" quandary.

> This would then be appealing because of weight (a small issue in the 
> mid/endgame, but not a total non-issue) 

No, seriously, it's a non-issue.  Look at how much a full set of
DSM/hat/gloves/boots/cloak weighs:

DSM: 40
hat: 50
gloves: 10
boots: 20
cloak: 10

That's 130 weight units, which is piddling.  By comparison, a tinning 
kit is 100, a crystal ball is 150, and a pick-axe is 100.  Two of these
can live in your bag of holding, but still -- 130 weight units in open
inventory is just not enough to matter unless you're trying to carry the
kitchen sink as well.

> the AK. The obvious alternative is to give the barb penalties for
> wearing armour instead of bonuses for not, but several other classes
> (Monk, wizard, all spellcasters in the early game) have already gone
> that route, so I was trying to think of something new.

And it's more appropriate anyway; a big hulking guy who's spent all his
time being "ZOG SMASH" is not going to have _that_ much trouble smashing
just because he puts on some leather armor or a breastplate.  Full plate
mail is likely to annoy him a bit, but...

